            <ArticleTitle>[The transrectus sheath preperitoneal procedure: a safe, effective and cheap surgical approach to inguinal hernia?].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>A6048</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The main complication of surgery for inguinal hernia is chronic postoperative pain. This is often reported following the Lichtenstein procedure. A new, open surgical technique for the repair of inguinal hernia has been developed. This procedure is called the transrectus sheath preperitoneal procedure (TREPP). At TREPP a lightweight mesh with a ring made of memory metal is introduced into the preperitoneal space through the transrectus sheath. The first results of this operative technique are very promising: short operation time, short learning curve and not many patients with chronic postoperative pain. In a randomised, multi-centre study which will start mid-2013 (ISRCTN18591339), the TREPP procedure is compared with the transinguinal preperitoneal procedure. The primary outcome measure of this study is chronic postoperative pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
